WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.410
I was very lucky to be
asked to come and speak

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.060
at MIT a couple of years ago.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.260
And so I had a chance
to spend a bit of time

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.840
and see this
institution that I'd

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:20.330
heard of all my life that
had produced invention,

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.610
which was always my interest.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.980
Rather than it
being about design,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.190
it was about ideas that
drive the world forward.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:33.650
And so it was thrilling when I
suddenly got, out of the blue,

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:38.729
this notification that I've
been chosen for this award.

00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:40.685
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.700
So when I was little,
I was interested

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.930
and wanted to be
an inventor, but it

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.590
was like that word wasn't
there in the world around me.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:10.980
If you studied painting or
studied food or writing,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.670
it seemed invention
was the key thing.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:18.720
But there was no way you could
study that, and so in a way,

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.190
I had to make up my own
version of an education

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.600
to make it work out somehow.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.870
And design seemed
the right word,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.320
and so it's been
exciting for me now

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.840
finding that
invention actually is

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.800
the nectar, is the thing
that's critical to progress.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.580
And MIT is a place which
is about invention.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:47.940
And so I suppose there was
a sense of slight coming

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.260
home, finding this place.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:57.000
Like Building 20 had, I think,
nine Nobel Prize winners

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.150
worked from Building
20 while it was there.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.980
And there seemed to
be a different level

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.700
of exceptional focus on
progress through ideas

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.590
and developing those ideas.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:19.290
And my grandfather had these
books on the great engineers,

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.670
and I found those very
clear that engineering

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.990
was about invention.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.810
Whether you call something
design or engineering

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.510
or architecture, to
me, it's all the same.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.290
And it felt like MIT
did put it all together

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.650
and was about ideas.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:41.150
So getting this letter
suddenly, saying

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:47.730
you're being offered this
incredible honor award,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.950
my heart went, ahh!

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:55.579
As a sort of link to somewhere
whose values I cherish.

00:02:55.579 --> 00:02:57.575
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.130
I found in design,
it's very interesting,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.710
the notion of being taught.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:57.480
Because in a sense, my
best tutors, professors,

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.580
were teachers who
didn't try to teach me

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:07.380
in a very demonstrative way.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.070
Actually, they
were facilitators.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:15.660
They allowed me to
jump and encouraged,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.630
and they facilitated,
but they certainly

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.840
didn't boss you around.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.920
The worst ones were the ones who
tried to tell you what to do,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.190
and they were the ones that
it was really important to not

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.930
take too seriously.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:36.120
And I think that spirit
matters now more than ever,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.730
when we have access to the
world's information in the way

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.090
that we do.

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:45.880
So I think we're in an
interesting time for teaching,

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:51.690
and I think any student
should have a healthy ability

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:55.215
to not listen to a tutor.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:02.100
And because you will always have
lots of people saying things,

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.200
and people come from
their own places,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:08.460
but the best, most
brilliant teachers

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.750
are watching and listening,
and it's not about them.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.422
And they're just
helping you to jump.

00:06:15.422 --> 00:06:16.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:15.060
I enjoyed in the interaction
that myself and my colleague

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.340
had with the students
was that we didn't

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.230
know who was an architect,
who was an engineer, who

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:22.680
was a biologist.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.850
We didn't know.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.380
You couldn't tell.

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:26.970
It didn't matter.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:32.910
What mattered was the intensity
of attention and focus

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.060
and journey and
hunger to find ideas.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.880
And that's what we
experienced, and that's

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.880
the experience I've
always had, and I've

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.950
found that the most
interesting people aren't

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.600
bothered by titles.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.880
They're just on a hunt and are
hungry and searching, working

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.890
with people, about a goal,
rather than official pompous

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:57.437
titling of things.

00:07:57.437 --> 00:07:59.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:43.570
We've been in a fascinating
time in the big arc of history

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.190
with the digital
revolution that's

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.820
happened over the
last two decades

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.030
and in my working
life, running a studio.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:57.790
And the advent of all
the layers of innovation

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.680
that have come from this
digital new dimensions

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.190
have meant that it seemed
like the physical would

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.260
be less important.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.950
And it's felt to me that
that's not the case.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.590
Actually, they have
been a platform

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:20.110
to lift and create even more
importance to the physical.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:25.110
And so actually, you can't get
away with lazy place-making

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.900
anymore when you can
buy things from home,

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.090
when you can study from home.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.650
And so people are making
their homes better.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.020
Why would you go somewhere?

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.600
You wouldn't go there
unless it means something

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.040
to your emotion.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.800
And in the past, there
could be lazy place-making,

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:46.080
because you had to go there
anyway to buy your bread

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:51.330
or to go and see a doctor
or to get a degree.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.450
And now that you don't have
to, places have to work harder.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.550
What are they for?

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.580
In the case of an
education institution,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.617
it's to bring students
together to have

00:11:01.617 --> 00:11:02.700
extraordinary interaction.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.700
Otherwise, they can just
stay at home and stay in bed,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.043
get their PhD.

00:11:08.043 --> 00:11:09.210
If only it were that simple.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:11.430
But it kind of is
getting that simple.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:17.580
But we're nothing without
connecting on a deep level,

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:21.930
and so places need to bring
people together in special ways

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:23.430
and mean something.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.090
And so the duplication that
was possible in the past

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:30.540
isn't possible anymore,
so this hyper-physicality

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.140
is what I feel we've
been immersing ourselves

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.110
in the studio like a research
project over the last two

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.913
decades, and it's
something that I think

00:11:39.913 --> 00:11:41.080
is more important than ever.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.560
And I was really
curious how the students

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:49.210
would respond with that
provocation thrown to them.

00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:51.510
And it was really interesting
what they came up with,

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:55.860
and it was exciting to see
the experiments and tests.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.610
It's really going some very
interesting directions.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:54.040
I wanted to thank the
Council, the students,

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.060
and the professors, and the
McDermott Prize itself--

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:02.440
family-- for this amazing award.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.620
For me, it's an
encouraging thing.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:09.170
It's not something
about the past.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.970
For me, it's an
encouragement that people

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:15.640
are interested in
the development work

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.900
that we've been doing as
a team and that there's

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:23.290
a sort of hand of
friendship reaching out

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:27.190
from a group of people
going, yeah, yeah.

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:28.810
Come on, keep going.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:31.510
Because I don't feel I'm
any finished article.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:34.270
I feel that we've just
got going as a studio.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:39.980
And as the students at MIT
must feel, to be at MIT

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:46.550
is a great sign of encouragement
and egging-on to do things.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:51.220
So I feel egged on by great
egger-onners and people who

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.820
themselves are being egged on.

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:57.070
So I feel less lonely,
because sometimes you

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.520
can feel by yourself
with your team,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:03.820
and to have that
hand of friendship--

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:05.680
which maybe sounds a
bit cheesy, but it's

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:10.060
a hand of friendship from
somewhere as incredible as MIT

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:15.430
and a family that was based on
innovation, like the McDermott

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:16.300
family--

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:17.890
is very exciting.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:20.330
And we're so grateful.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:22.570
I'll speak on
behalf of my studio.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.370
I'm nothing without my studio.

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

